        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Picks elements out of an array by a list of positions, in any order and as often as you like. np.take 4 0 2 of 10 20 30 40 50 gives 50 10 30.\n\nUse it to reorder (a joint list into another skeleton's order), to select a subset (the arm joints from a full body), to duplicate, or to look values up: a list of indices from np.argsort, np.digitize or np.unique's inverse, taken from a table of colours, notes or names.\n\nAlong axis 0 of a 2-D array it picks whole rows; along axis 1, whole columns.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.take <indices: ints ...>\nDefault: no indices (send them to the indices inlet).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.take 0 2 4\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array to pick from (a list is turned into one). Every input is picked from and sent.\n\nindices:\nThe positions, replacing any given as arguments. Held: send them first. A negative position counts from the end: -1 is the last.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way positions count: 0 (the default) along the first axis - single elements of a list, whole rows of a 2-D array; 1 columns.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node: what happens to positions past the end. clip (the default) gives the last element (or the first, for positions before the start);\nwrap goes round again from the start, so position 5 of 3 elements is position 2.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntaken:\nThe picked elements, in the order of the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: positions 4 0 2 of 10 20 30 40 50 are 50 10 30.\n\nBottom: the positions come from a message instead: -1 is the last element, and 0 1 twice shows the same element can be taken more than once. A metro re-sends the array five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.[] takes a slice - a run of neighbouring elements. np.argsort, np.argmax and np.digitize make lists of positions; t.take is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Picks elements out of an array by a list of positions, in any order and as often as you like. np.take 4 0 2 of 10 20 30 40 50 gives 50 10 30.\n\nUse it to reorder (a joint list into another skeleton's order), to select a subset (the arm joints from a full body), to duplicate, or to look values up: a list of indices from np.argsort, np.digitize or np.unique's inverse, taken from a table of colours, notes or names.\n\nAlong axis 0 of a 2-D array it picks whole rows; along axis 1, whole columns.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.take <indices: ints ...>\nDefault: no indices (send them to the indices inlet).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.take 0 2 4\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array to pick from (a list is turned into one). Every input is picked from and sent.\n\nindices:\nThe positions, replacing any given as arguments. Held: send them first. A negative position counts from the end: -1 is the last.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way positions count: 0 (the default) along the first axis - single elements of a list, whole rows of a 2-D array; 1 columns.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node: what happens to positions past the end. clip (the default) gives the last element (or the first, for positions before the start);\nwrap goes round again from the start, so position 5 of 3 elements is position 2.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntaken:\nThe picked elements, in the order of the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: positions 4 0 2 of 10 20 30 40 50 are 50 10 30.\n\nBottom: the positions come from a message instead: -1 is the last element, and 0 1 twice shows the same element can be taken more than once. A metro re-sends the array five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.[] takes a slice - a run of neighbouring elements. np.argsort, np.argmax and np.digitize make lists of positions; t.take is the PyTorch version."
        },
